Neverball and Neverputt for generated9

Native C ports of Neverball 1.6.0 and its minigolf companion. See UPSTREAM,
LICENSE.md and the preserved notices for attribution and licensing.

Both games share one native platform: libdraw/libevent for rio and input,
libttf for text, system PNG/JPEG readers, and a PCM mixer over /dev/audio.
The original physics, level formats, scores and replay code are retained.
Graphics use the classic GL subset in libtinygl and VirGL. There is no
SDL, C++, Mesa or foreign compiler in the guest.

Prepare data on the host:

    ./boot/getneverball

Then inside generated9:

    cd /sys/src/libgpu
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/libtinygl
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/games/neverball
    mk install
    mk data
    games/neverball
    games/neverputt

The build needs no game data; playing does. The installer verifies the
pinned original archive and keeps its asset licenses. mk data compiles all
missing .map files with native mapc and decodes Ogg tracks with audio/oggdec.
Remove an affected .sol or .pcm to rebuild it after changing its source.
The first conversion of all 407 maps takes several minutes.

The shared source and data directories are now named neverball. For an
existing checkout, move sys/games/lib/neverputt to sys/games/lib/neverball
on the host before mk data; already compiled holes and audio are reused.
boot/getneverputt remains an installer alias. The games/neverputt command
and $home/lib/neverputt settings path are unchanged. Neverball keeps its
own settings, scores, replays and screenshots in $home/lib/neverball.

Boot the current pc64 kernel with a VirGL device as described in boot/GPU.md.
TinyGL evicts cached GPU texture copies under resource pressure, retaining
images in guest memory. Sound requires a supported device, for example QEMU
intel-hda with hda-duplex and a host audio backend. /dev/audio uses 44.1 kHz
stereo signed 16-bit little-endian PCM. The games report unavailable audio.
Only one application can open this audio device for output at a time.

See neverball(1) and neverputt(1) for controls and options. Native menus omit
display modes owned by rio and unsupported graphics features. Translations,
gamepads, HMD, reflections and multisampling are not ported. Six general
user clip planes in TinyGL support Neverball's transparent-ball rendering.

Some maps in the original archive reference missing material images.
mapc reports them and uses unit texture coordinates; the original material
loader supplies a default material. The native compiler also clamps rounded
normal dot products before acos. The camera fade handles a zero duration
explicitly instead of relying on division by zero and subsequent clamping.

Validation uses amd64 QEMU/KVM with four CPUs, 4 GiB RAM, VirGL and HDA.
All 407 original maps compile natively. Tests cover native graphics and
animation, menus, text input, gameplay, pause, resize and clean shutdown;
this does not imply every level has been played to completion.

The mixer regression test requires /dev/audio but no assets:

    cd /sys/src/games/neverball/test
    mk test

It checks repeated initialization, pause/resume, shutdown while paused,
missing background music and joining the mixer without consuming another
child's wait message. Run it when no other application owns /dev/audio.
Exit handlers also release the mixer if a requested level cannot be opened.
